# Cloudflare local-dev secrets for the E2E suite ONLY — selected by `CLOUDFLARE_ENV=e2e`, which
# `playwright.config.ts` sets on its `webServer`. Wrangler's `loadDotDevDotVars` tries
# `.dev.vars.<env>` FIRST and falls back to `.dev.vars`, so this file REPLACES `.dev.vars` wholesale
# for an e2e run — every key the app needs has to be restated here.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (impl-review F1). `webServer.env` cannot make the app see a non-billable vendor
# key: @astrojs/cloudflare re-reads `.dev.vars` at `astro:config:done` and the workerd bindings come
# from wrangler's own read of it, so a placeholder passed through the process env is overwritten by
# the developer's real key. Verified empirically — with placeholders in `webServer.env`, the Worker
# still read the real 35- and 73-character keys. Owning the secrets FILE is the only lever that wins.
#
# WHY IT IS COMMITTED, unlike `.dev.vars`. It holds no secret: the Supabase values are the CLI's fixed,
# publicly documented local demo keys (identical on every machine, already used by CI — see README
# "CI"), and the vendor values are deliberate non-credentials. Committing it also removes the failure
# mode that worried us most: wrangler falls back to `.dev.vars` SILENTLY when this file is missing, so
# an absent file would quietly restore real keys. `playwright.config.ts` additionally refuses to start
# when this file is gone, so the fallback can never happen unnoticed.
#
# It is NOT covered by `.gitignore`'s `.dev.vars` entry (line 57 is an exact name, not a glob). If that
# pattern is ever broadened to `.dev.vars*`, re-add an explicit `!.dev.vars.e2e` negation.

# --- local Supabase stack: the CLI's fixed demo keys (`npx supabase status`) ---
SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321
SUPABASE_KEY=eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJpc3MiOiJzdXBhYmFzZS1kZW1vIiwicm9sZSI6ImFub24iLCJleHAiOjE5ODM4MTI5OTZ9.CRXP1A7WOeoJeXxjNni43kdQwgnWNReilDMblYTn_I0
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJpc3MiOiJzdXBhYmFzZS1kZW1vIiwicm9sZSI6InNlcnZpY2Vfcm9sZSIsImV4cCI6MTk4MzgxMjk5Nn0.EGIM96RAZx35lJzdJsyH-qQwv8Hdp7fsn3W0YpN81IU

# --- paid vendors: deliberately NOT credentials ---
# Supadata is defeated by DATA (specs pre-seed both caches through the same RPCs `generate.ts` calls),
# so a correct run never reaches it. These values are what makes an INCORRECT run — a cache miss from a
# seeding bug — fail with a vendor 401 instead of billing the account. They mirror the integration
# suite's `fetch-firewall.ts`, which pins the same two keys to placeholders for the same reason.
SUPADATA_API_KEY=e2e-placeholder-not-a-real-supadata-key
# OpenRouter is defeated by CODE (the `E2E_FAKE_LLM` module alias in `astro.config.mjs`), so this is
# the second lock on a door that is already bolted — it only matters if that alias ever fails to apply.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=e2e-placeholder-not-a-real-openrouter-key

# --- Sentry: NO DSN HERE, ON PURPOSE ---
# The other paid boundaries above are neutralised with a non-credential; this one is neutralised by
# ABSENCE, because that is the stronger move available: an unset DSN leaves the Sentry SDK
# uninitialised, so there is no transport to mis-fire, and no placeholder DSN that a future SDK
# version might decide to parse and attempt. An e2e run must never file an issue against the
# operator's project — the run is not an incident.
#
# This file is what makes that true for the WORKER: it REPLACES `.dev.vars` wholesale for an e2e run
# (see the header), so a `SENTRY_DSN` in your own `.dev.vars` cannot be inherited here — deleting the
# variable from this file is not an omission, it is the deletion.
#
# It does NOT cover the BROWSER half. `PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN` is a CLIENT variable, inlined into the
# bundle at BUILD time from the build process's env, which wrangler's secrets file never touches. A
# DSN sitting in your `.env` would reach the build through `npm run test:e2e`'s
# `--env-file-if-exists=.env`. That gap is closed one level up, by the config-load guard in
# `playwright.config.ts` that refuses to start when either variable is set.
